As for the performance difference between `splice()` and `filter()`, here is some information based on benchmarks conducted by various sources:

- Both `splice()` and `filter()` modify the original array in-place, so they don't create any additional memory overhead.

- However, `splice()` changes the length of the array, while `filter()` returns a completely new array with the elements that passed the given condition. As such, when it comes to performance, `filter()` may perform better if you need to remove only a few elements from a long array because creating a brand-new array with just the necessary elements takes less time than modifying indices of the existing array through `splice()`. On the other hand, if you need to remove many elements from a relatively small array, `splice()` might be slightly faster since it works directly on the original array.

Here are some approximate numbers from different benchmark tests:

- In Chrome V8 engine, `filter()` outperforms `splice()` when removing around 10% or fewer items from the array. When removing greater percentages of items, e.g., 50%, `splice()` starts performing better due to modifying an array in-place. Source: https://jsbench.me/9uqfvsd6n7/1/
- In Node.js v14.17.0, `filter()` significantly beats `splice()` across several benchmarks that involve removing varying amounts (e.g., 10% to 90%) of elements from arrays of different lengths. The margin of improvement increases with larger input sizes. Source: https://benchmarksgame-team.pages.debian.net/benchmarksgame/single/platform=node_js_14_lts-npm_i_babel-cli-browserify_gh-pages/node_v14.html#test=string+filtering+nanosort&data=basic

