To draw a chessboard with a given FEN notation using LaTeX, you will need the following:

1. The `chessboard` package: This provides the `\newchessgame` command which parses the FEN string and creates a game object.

2. TikZ or PGF for creating the graphics.

3. A custom style for the pieces.

Here's an example code snippet:

```tex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage{chessboard}
\usepackage{tikz}

\definecolor{white}{RGB}{255,255,255}
\definecolor{black}{RGB}{0,0,0}
\definecolor{darkgray}{RGB}{51,51,51}
\definecolor{textblack}{RGB}{64,64,64}

% Define piece style
\tikzset{
    knight/.style={shape=rectangle, draw=textblack, fill=blue!30, minimum size=.6cm},
    bishop/.style={shape=rectangle, draw=textblack, fill=green!30, minimum size=.6cm},
    rook/.style={shape=rectangle, draw=textblack, fill=brown!30, minimum size=.6cm},
    queen/.style={shape=rectangle, draw=textblack, fill=red!30, minimum size=.6cm},
    king/.style={shape=circle, draw=textblack, fill=yellow!20, minimum width=.8cm},
    pawn/.style={shape=coordinate},
}

\begin{document}

% Parse the FEN notation
\chessboardsetup{notation=.,light squares=top,move marks=false,moveset=false,xray=false,piece styles=knight,bishop,rook,queen,king,pawn}
\newchessgame{\fen}{r1bqkbnr/ppp1ppp/2n2n1/3p4/2P2p1/pP1P3/PP1PPPPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq 0 7}

% Draw the chessboard
\chessboard{\fen}

\end{document}
```

Replace `\fen` by your desired FEN notation. In this example, we use a dark/light color scheme where white spaces are colored light gray and black spaces are colored dark gray, and piece colors follow a standard set. For a custom color scheme, adjust the `xcolor` settings accordingly. 

