Sure thing! The DIY Laser Harp is a unique and engaging way to use lasers and sensors to create a beautiful and interactive musical instrument.

Here are the steps to build a simple version:

Materials:

- An assortment of 5mm diffused red, green, blue, purple or white lasers (with appropriate power supply voltage)
- IR photoresistors
- A clear acrylic sheet (size depends on your preference), thick enough to allow laser beams to pass through
- Metal rods or wires (at least 8 pieces total)
- Breadboard
- Jumper wires
- Arduino Uno / Mega Board
- Adafruit NeoPixel Ring or similar ws2812b led ring (optional)

Instructions:

1. Cut the acrylic sheet into desired size and shape, making sure the backside is flat and smooth to allow all beams to pass through evenly.
2. Affix metal rods or wires vertically from both sides of the acrylic base, around the edges or wherever suitable according to your design, providing stable support for the beams.
3. Connect each photodiode sensor to one of the IR-sensitive photoresistors using jumper wires.
4. Place the photoresistor modules facing downwards where their light sensors face directly towards the bottom side of the acrylic sheet underneath the supported metal rods.
5. Connect each photoresistor to ground and analog pin (A0-A7). 
6. Power up the Arduino and connect it to your computer.
7. Create a sketch for the laser harp with code using any beginner-friendly Arduino libraries like "LaserHarpoon" available online or download and install WS2812B NeoPixel Library & Adafruit NeoPixel library.

   Here's a sample code adapted from Arduino IDE, which controls the light and sound aspects of this project:
```C++
#include <Adafruit_NeoPixel.h>

#define PIN        12 //NeoPixel Pin Number

Adafruit_NeoPixel strip = Adafruit_NeoPixel(8, PIN, NEO_GRB + NEO_KHZ800);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  strip.begin();
}

void loop() {
  if(digitalRead(A0) == HIGH){ //For example, If Green Beam is Broken
    Serial.println("Green");
    setBeamColor(0, GREEN); //sets corresponding LED color Green (R=0, G=255, B=0)
  }
  else if(digitalRead(A1) == HIGH){ //If Red Beam is Broken
    Serial.println("Red");
    setBeamColor(1, RED); //sets corresponding LED color Red (R=255, G=0, B=0)
  }
  //Repeat for remaining beams
}

//Function to set Color of specific LED on NeoPixel Ring
void setBeamColor(uint8_t index, uint32_t color){
  strip.setPixelColor(index, color);
  strip.show();
}
```

Note that this is only a simple overview of the general concept involved, but you may need additional circuits or modifications based on your preferences.

Additional resources and tutorials on constructing an advanced version of this project can be found on various websites, YouTube channels, forums or from professionals in the field.

Enjoy crafting your very own DIY Laser Harp!

